    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get that it serves a function, but don't you think that by focusing so much on the message, the actual quality of the work suffers? like, if a piece is just a political statement, does it even matter if it's well-made or aesthetically pleasing anymore? it seems like we're trading artistic excellence for just being loud.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The notion that aesthetic quality is irrelevant is a romanticized view that ignores the power of effective communication. A poorly executed piece, no matter how righteous its message, is simply noise, whereas a powerful, well-crafted piece drives genuine dialogue. The market and critics are increasingly recognizing that the most impactful contemporary art successfully *integrates* a potent message with undeniable formal strength.",
      "source": "target",
